  "summary": "Describe people and things with who, which and that: الَّذِي, الَّتِي and الَّذِينَ, and talk about books, writers and prizes.",
  "intro": "Daniel and Layla go to a book fair. To say 'the man who…' or 'the book that…', Arabic uses a relative word that agrees with the noun before it: الَّذِي after a masculine noun, الَّتِي after a feminine noun or a plural of things, and الَّذِينَ after a group of people. It is used only after a noun with 'the': الْكَاتِبُ الَّذِي أَخَذَ الْجَائِزَة (the writer who won the prize). After a noun without 'the' there is no relative word: عِنْدِي صَدِيق يَكْتُبُ قِصَصًا (I have a friend who writes stories).",
  "objectives": [
    "Join two sentences with الَّذِي, الَّتِي and الَّذِينَ",
    "Choose the right relative word for a man, a woman, things and people",
    "Leave the relative word out after a noun without 'the'",
    "Talk about books, novels, writers and prizes"
  ],
